## **2. Automatically compare input, filter and output sections**

We will use Perl since it is already working on your Mac. No additional software installation is required.

The next script will classify the 34 different pipelines into:

- **FILTER_CHANGED:** Review and update the filter configuration.
- **INPUT_OUTPUT_ONLY:** Filters match; review routing changes and ignore intentional environment-specific differences.
- **MANUAL_REVIEW:** Configuration formatting prevents reliable automatic section extraction.

It will also generate a CSV report so that you can track the required changes.

### **Step 2.1: Navigate to your existing directory**

```
cd ~/lma-pipeline-comparison

umask 077
```

### **Step 2.2: Create the comparison script**

Copy and paste this entire block into your Mac terminal:

```
cat > compare_sections.pl <<'PERL'
use strict;
use warnings;
use JSON::PP;

sub load_json {
    my ($file) = @_;
    open my $fh, '<:raw', $file or die "$file: $!";
    local $/;
    return decode_json(<$fh>);
}

sub sections {
    my ($config) = @_;

    $config =~ s/\r\n/\n/g;

    my %result = (
        input  => [],
        filter => [],
        output => []
    );

    my $section;
    my @block;
    my $found = 0;

    for my $line (split /\n/, $config) {

        if (!defined $section) {

            next if $line =~ /^\s*(#.*)?$/;

            if ($line =~ /^(input|filter|output)\s*\{\s*(?:#.*)?$/) {

                $section = $1;
                @block = ($line);
                $found++;

            } else {
                return undef;
            }

        } else {

            if ($line =~ /^\}\s*(?:#.*)?$/) {

                push @block, $line;

                push @{$result{$section}},
                    join("\n", @block);

                undef $section;
                @block = ();

            } elsif ($line =~ /^[^ \t#]/) {

                return undef;

            } else {

                push @block, $line;

            }
        }
    }

    return undef if defined $section || !$found;

    return \%result;
}

my $prod = load_json("com-lma-prod-fixed.json");
my $dev  = load_json("non-lma-dev-fixed.json");

open my $csv, '>:encoding(UTF-8)',
    'section_comparison.csv' or die $!;

print $csv "Pipeline_ID,Status,Input,Filter,Output\n";

my %all = map { $_ => 1 } (keys %$prod, keys %$dev);
my %summary;

for my $id (sort keys %all) {

    die "Unexpected pipeline ID: $id\n"
        unless $id =~ /^[A-Za-z0-9_-]+$/;

    my ($status, $input, $filter, $output) =
        ('', 'N/A', 'N/A', 'N/A');

    if (!exists $dev->{$id}) {

        $status = 'MISSING_IN_DEV';

    } elsif (!exists $prod->{$id}) {

        $status = 'DEV_ONLY';

    } elsif ($prod->{$id}{pipeline}
        eq $dev->{$id}{pipeline}) {

        $status = 'MATCHED';
        ($input, $filter, $output) =
            ('SAME', 'SAME', 'SAME');

    } else {

        my $p = sections($prod->{$id}{pipeline});
        my $d = sections($dev->{$id}{pipeline});

        if (!$p || !$d) {

            $status = 'MANUAL_REVIEW';

        } else {

            $input = encode_json($p->{input})
                eq encode_json($d->{input})
                ? 'SAME' : 'DIFFERENT';

            $filter = encode_json($p->{filter})
                eq encode_json($d->{filter})
                ? 'SAME' : 'DIFFERENT';

            $output = encode_json($p->{output})
                eq encode_json($d->{output})
                ? 'SAME' : 'DIFFERENT';

            if ($filter eq 'DIFFERENT') {

                $status = 'FILTER_CHANGED';

            } elsif ($input eq 'DIFFERENT'
                || $output eq 'DIFFERENT') {

                $status = 'INPUT_OUTPUT_ONLY';

            } else {

                $status = 'SECTION_TEXT_MATCHED';

            }
        }
    }

    print $csv join(",",
        $id, $status, $input, $filter, $output), "\n";

    $summary{$status}++;

    print "$id : $status\n";
}

close $csv;

print "\n===== COMPARISON SUMMARY =====\n";

for my $status (sort keys %summary) {
    print "$status : $summary{$status}\n";
}

print "\nReport: section_comparison.csv\n";
PERL
```

### **Step 2.3: Execute the script**

```
perl compare_sections.pl
```

Open the generated report:

```
open section_comparison.csv
```

## **3. Expected comparison report**

The report will look similar to this (illustrative results):

| Pipeline ID           | Input | Filter    | Output    | Status            |
| --------------------- | ----- | --------- | --------- | ----------------- |
| beats-filebeat-output | Same  | Same      | Different | INPUT_OUTPUT_ONLY |
| beats-apm             | Same  | Different | Same      | FILTER_CHANGED    |
| beats-gitlab-logs     | Same  | Different | Different | FILTER_CHANGED    |
| beats-aem-logs        | Same  | Same      | Same      | MATCHED           |
| beats-cds-logs        | N/A   | N/A       | N/A       | MISSING_IN_DEV    |

The script compares section text, not semantic equivalence. Comments and indentation changes within sections can still appear as differences. It deliberately sends nonstandard block layouts for manual review rather than guessing.

## **4. Extract only the pipelines requiring filter review**

Execute:

```
grep ',FILTER_CHANGED,' section_comparison.csv
```

To display only pipeline names:

```
grep ',FILTER_CHANGED,' section_comparison.csv \
| cut -d, -f1
```

To identify pipelines where only inputs or outputs differ:

```
grep ',INPUT_OUTPUT_ONLY,' section_comparison.csv
```

To identify configurations requiring manual review:

```
grep ',MANUAL_REVIEW,' section_comparison.csv
```

This should significantly reduce the number of pipelines you need to compare manually.

